  "summary_proposal": {
    "field": "summary",
    "value": "Three sources compare target-nomination methods against measured CRISPR screen outcomes. All are retrospective replays of published screens: a method picks genes, and the screen's existing measurement is looked up. Non-hits are genes the screen assayed that did not meet its significance criterion; genes the screen never assayed are outside the candidate pool and are never counted as negatives, so none of these results says anything about an untested target. Roohani et al. 2025, the developers of the agent they test, report hit ratio after five rounds of 128 genes on six screens. For their best agent (Claude 3.5 Sonnet) the all-gene hit ratio was 0.095 on the interferon-gamma screen, 0.104 on interleukin-2, 0.130 on CAR-T proliferation, 0.326 on lysosomal choline recycling, 0.042 on T-cell inhibition resistance and 0.066 on tau level, against random selection at 0.037, 0.031, 0.033, 0.160, 0.036 and 0.034. Conventional baselines were closer on some screens: Coreset reached 0.102 on interleukin-2 and 0.047 on T-cell inhibition resistance, above the agent's 0.042 on the latter. Their human pathway baseline was at or below random on five of the six screens, for example 0.025 against 0.034 on tau level. Gupta et al. 2025, independent of the agent's authors, replayed five of the same screens and report cumulative hits. Running the same agent with its experimental feedback replaced by randomly permuted outcomes changed little: with a Llama-3.1-8B backbone the agent scored 39.4 against 37 with permuted feedback on interleukin-2, and 44 against 51 on interferon-gamma; the permuted variant scored at least as high on three of the five screens, and the same pattern held with a Claude 3.5 Sonnet backbone. That source concludes the models it tested do not perform in-context experimental design. Classical sequential designs over the same embeddings were sometimes far stronger: Gaussian process optimisation reached 147.8 cumulative hits on interleukin-2 against the agent's 39.4, from a printed ground-truth hit count of 654. De Brouwer et al. 2026, a preprint, asked 16 systems to rank 100 genes for each of 571 described screens. On the test cohort the best system was an evolved ensemble of language models at 0.1631 adjusted NDCG@100 and the best single model 0.1570, against 0.1334 for a baseline that ranks genes by how often they are hits in training screens of the same phenotype, 0.0565 for a predictor trained on the benchmark, and 0.2918 for a retrieval upper bound that uses the answer. Every system scored lower on the 19 screens published after the models' training cutoff, the ensemble falling to 0.1088, which that source reads as evidence that part of the apparent skill on older screens is prior exposure to their literature. No source here tests a prospective validation batch, a target's usefulness, dependency, tractability or therapeutic window, or whether modulating any target benefits patients.",
